  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe center of surface high pressure is expected to shift east\ntoday, allowing the impact of another disturbance late tonight\nand into the day on Thursday. A strong shortwave trough will \npass through the region as a surface cold front pushes across \nthe area on Thursday. \n\nGetting into the details and parameters, the timing and hi-res\ncams seems to suggest a broken line weakening and dissipating in\ncentral OH overnight. The front approaches during the day on\nThursday and the broken line of showers and thunderstorms\ndevelops again around Allegheny County and east. This area\ndevelops with the frontal forcing and heating of the day. The\namount of dry air in place will make this an environment where\ndamaging wind would be the main threat but a few instances of\nhail is possible for Thursday afternoon and evening. The\nensembles have been pretty consistent of 1500 - 2000 J/Kg of SB\nCAPE ahead of the front. The suggestion of even the 75th\npercentile, although unlikely, still only bumps up the\ninstability to around 1750 - 2250 J/Kg, so a reasonable threat\nthat will be widely scattered. Timing and the loss of heating \ngives the convection a pumpkin time of around 01Z to 02Z.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe cold front is expected to exit the region Thursday\nevening/night, with showers and thunderstorms ending. Dry\nweather should then return Friday through the weekend as surface\nhigh pressure builds under broad upper troughing aloft. The \nnext upper trough is progged to approach the region Tuesday,\nthough ensembles differ on the strength of the trough. Shower\nand thunderstorm chances should increase with the approaching\ntrough.",
